Rem Net demo: a whole client/server conversation over TCP/IP, both ends in
Rem one program on the loopback address. Run with
Rem     mill run --allow-net tst/net-demo.shoddy
Rem (the --allow-net flag is required — the network is a gated capability).
Rem
Rem It stands up a server, connects a client to it, and trades two
Rem messages: the client sends a line, the server shouts it back in
Rem upper case. Everything is single-threaded because the socket words
Rem never block — after Connect the server's WaitAccept finds the pending
Rem client at once, and WaitRecv polls (around Sleep) until a line lands.

Include "../machines/net.shoddy"
Include "../machines/str.shoddy"

Def Main()
    Print("Shoddy net demo - a loopback conversation")
    Let port = 7420
    Let srv = Listen(port)
    Print("listening on 127.0.0.1:" & Str(port))

    Let client = Connect("127.0.0.1", port)
    Let server = WaitAccept(srv)
    Print("connected; server sees peer " & Peer(server))
    Print("")

    Chat(client, server, "hello")
    Chat(client, server, "shoddy speaks tcp")

    Close(client)
    Close(server)
    Close(srv)
    Print("")
    Print("done")

Rem One round trip: the client sends a line, the server reads it and
Rem echoes it upper-cased, the client reads the reply. Chomp drops the
Rem \r\n that SendLine adds, so the printed transcript is clean.
Def Chat(client As Number, server As Number, msg As String)
    SendLine(client, msg)
    Let heard = Chomp(WaitRecv(server))
    SendLine(server, Upper(heard))
    Let reply = Chomp(WaitRecv(client))
    Print("  client sent: " & msg)
    Print("  server said: " & reply)

Rem Strip the trailing line ending a text protocol puts on each line.
Def Chomp(s As String) As String
    Replace(Replace(s, "\r", ""), "\n", "")
